I.7 QR factorisation and stable least squares
Exercises from Chapter 30.
1. \(r_{11} = \lVert(3, 4)\rVert = 5\) and \(\vect{q}_1 = (0.6, 0.8)\T\). Then \(r_{12} = \vect{q}_1 \cdot (1, 2)\T = 2.2\), and \((1, 2)\T - 2.2\vect{q}_1 = (-0.32, 0.24)\T\), of length \(r_{22} = 0.4\), so \(\vect{q}_2 = (-0.8, 0.6)\T\): \[
\M{Q} = \mat{0.6 & -0.8 \\ 0.8 & 0.6},
\qquad
\M{R} = \mat{5 & 2.2 \\ 0 & 0.4}.
\] (Software may return both with opposite signs.)
Back to exercise 1
2. \(\lVert\vect{x}\rVert = 3\), so \(\vect{v} = (1, 2, 2)\T + 3(1, 0, 0)\T = (4, 2, 2)\T\), with \(\vect{v}\T\vect{v} = 24\). Then \[
\M{H} = \M{I} - \frac{1}{12}\mat{16 & 8 & 8 \\ 8 & 4 & 4 \\ 8 & 4 & 4} = \frac{1}{3}\mat{-1 & -2 & -2 \\ -2 & 2 & -1 \\ -2 & -1 & 2},
\qquad
\M{H}\vect{x} = \frac{1}{3}\mat{-1 - 4 - 4 \\ -2 + 4 - 2 \\ -2 - 2 + 4} = \mat{-3 \\ 0 \\ 0}.
\]
Back to exercise 2
3. Write \(\M{H} = \M{I} - 2\vect{u}\vect{u}\T\) with \(\vect{u} = \vect{v}/\lVert\vect{v}\rVert\), a unit vector. \(\M{H}\T = \M{I} - 2(\vect{u}\vect{u}\T)\T = \M{I} - 2\vect{u}\vect{u}\T = \M{H}\), so it is symmetric. Then \[
\M{H}\T\M{H} = \M{H}^2 = \M{I} - 4\vect{u}\vect{u}\T + 4\vect{u}(\vect{u}\T\vect{u})\vect{u}\T = \M{I},
\] since \(\vect{u}\T\vect{u} = 1\), so it is orthogonal. Finally, \(\M{H}\vect{v} = \vect{v} - 2\vect{u}(\vect{u}\T\vect{v}) = \vect{v} - 2\vect{v} = -\vect{v}\), because \(\vect{u}(\vect{u}\T\vect{v}) = \vect{v}\) (\(\vect{v}\) is along \(\vect{u}\)).
Back to exercise 3
4. \(\M{A} = \mat{1 & -1 \\ 1 & 0 \\ 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 2}\) and \(\vect{b} = (0, 1, 1, 3)\T\). Gram–Schmidt: \(r_{11} = 2\), \(\vect{q}_1 = \tfrac{1}{2}(1, 1, 1, 1)\T\); \(r_{12} = \tfrac{1}{2}(-1 + 0 + 1 + 2) = 1\); \(\vect{a}_2 - \vect{q}_1 = (-1.5, -0.5, 0.5, 1.5)\T\), of length \(r_{22} = \sqrt{5}\). Then \(\M{Q}\T\vect{b} = \bigl(2.5, \; 4.5/\sqrt{5}\bigr)\), and back substitution gives \(c_1 = 4.5/5 = 0.9\) and \(2c_0 + 0.9 = 2.5\), so \(c_0 = 0.8\). The line is \(y = 0.8 + 0.9t\).
Back to exercise 4
5. With \(\M{A} = \M{U}\M{\Sigma}\M{V}\T\), \(\M{A}\T\M{A} = \M{V}\M{\Sigma}\T\M{\Sigma}\M{V}\T\), which is an SVD of \(\M{A}\T\M{A}\) with singular values \(\sigma_i^2\). So \(\kappa_2(\M{A}\T\M{A}) = \sigma_1^2/\sigma_n^2 = \kappa_2(\M{A})^2\).
Back to exercise 5
6. Extend \(\M{Q}\) to a square orthogonal matrix \([\M{Q} \; \M{Q}_2]\). Multiplying by an orthogonal matrix preserves length, so \[
\lVert\vect{b} - \M{A}\vect{x}\rVert^2 = \lVert\M{Q}\T\vect{b} - \M{R}\vect{x}\rVert^2 + \lVert\M{Q}_2\T\vect{b}\rVert^2.
\] The first term is zero at \(\hat{\vect{x}}\), so the residual is \(\lVert\M{Q}_2\T\vect{b}\rVert^2 = \lVert\vect{b}\rVert^2 - \lVert\M{Q}\T\vect{b}\rVert^2\), again because the full orthogonal matrix preserves the length of \(\vect{b}\).
Back to exercise 6
7.

PythonMATLAB



import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

def classical_gs(A):
    Q = np.zeros(A.shape)
    for j in range(A.shape[1]):
        v = A[:, j] - Q[:, :j] @ (Q[:, :j].T @ A[:, j])   # project the original column
        Q[:, j] = v / np.linalg.norm(v)
    return Q

def modified_gs(A):
    Q = A.astype(float).copy()
    for j in range(A.shape[1]):
        Q[:, j] /= np.linalg.norm(Q[:, j])
        for k in range(j + 1, A.shape[1]):              # update the remaining columns now
            Q[:, k] -= (Q[:, j] @ Q[:, k]) * Q[:, j]
    return Q

t = np.linspace(0, 1, 50)
conds, loss = [], {"classical": [], "modified": [], "Householder": []}
for n in [4, 6, 8, 10, 12]:
    A = np.vander(t, n, increasing=True)
    conds.append(np.linalg.cond(A))
    for name, Q in [("classical", classical_gs(A)), ("modified", modified_gs(A)),
                    ("Householder", np.linalg.qr(A)[0])]:
        loss[name].append(np.linalg.norm(Q.T @ Q - np.eye(n)))

plt.figure(figsize=(6, 3.5))
for name, values in loss.items():
    plt.loglog(conds, values, "o-", label=name)
plt.xlabel("condition number of A")
plt.ylabel("‖QᵀQ − I‖")
plt.legend()
plt.show()










